    # messages claim. The record script's own test is negative.log's, not this
    # log's. Each check is evaluated once (specs/0001: a check with more than one
    # condition lives in one function that returns yes or no).
    #
    # WRITTEN FOR M1 part 7 P2 from part 7 P1's final test script (the script
    # printed in records/logs/m1p7-p2/test.log), audited against specs/0001
    # "Rules for every phase", specs/0013's rulings, specs/0014's P1 rulings and
    # P1 verification rulings, and grepped for P1's names, paths and subjects.
    # What differs from that one, and why:
    #  1. The phase, the log directory and the status terms are part 7 P2's
    #     (records/logs/m1p7-p2); the previous phase's figures and names are read
    #     from records/logs/m1p7-p2/test.log.
    #  2. The commits by role: the P1 record commit (the base, by its subject
    #     "M1 part 7 P1 record:"), the doc commit and the two code commits, each
    #     by its full subject, all beginning "M1 part 7 P2: ", checked against
    #     the phase commit's parents; every commit in the range is checked to
    #     carry the prefix. No id is typed.
    #  3. The case map (P1 verification ruling 7): the spec's parenthetical
    #     "(names equal, values 1 and 1)" maps to the "one attribute's value
    #     changed" fixture of elements_and_attributes_added_removed_and_changed,
    #     and "all four at once" is its own row; the four single changes each
    #     have a row. Ruling 6's two fixtures join the map.
    #  4. Ruling 8: the probe over p1 to p6 (with p7 as the control) and over
    #     passthrough's copies tests (with the run() probe as the control) is
    #     kept. P1's part 2, p3.rs reverted to the base, measured P1's helper
    #     change; P2 changes no helper and no pre-existing test, so it is
    #     dropped, and the test files the phase changed are printed and checked
    #     to be tests/p7.rs alone.
    #  5. M1 to M11 are kept and re-run at the phase commit; M12 is new: the
    #     attribute value hashed as written instead of through normalized_value,
    #     expected to fail an_attribute_cr_lf_against_an_lf_compares_equal. M9
    #     on the host stays MEASURED. P1 verification ruling 4: M9's failing
    #     assertion on i686 is printed as the run prints it, unfiltered.
    #  6. The claims blocks read the two P2 code commits' messages.
    #  7. P1 verification ruling 3: every root the log uses has its own root
    #     line: <repo>, <home>, <scratch>/p2 (the script and its build directory
    #     <scratch>/p2/target), <scratch>/w and <scratch>/mut; each pair is
    #     checked distinct.
    #  8. Everything else is P1's final form (attempt 2 at its second code
    #     commit): lines 1 to 3 are the command, the invocation and "about:";
    #     the three shape greps with controls; the by-name comparison with the
    #     previous log; the added and ignored tests derived from the source; the
    #     comparison of the two targets. No count the phase can grow is typed.
    #     None of the three shapes specs/0001 forbids appears here.
    #  9. Attempt 2 (attempt 1 named in the rerun line): the first code
    #     commit's message wraps "The test results are in this phase's
    #     test.log" across two lines, so attempt 1's count over the message's
    #     lines found 0; the message is now read as one line (newlines to
    #     spaces) for that claim, with a control. The git grep for users of
    #     xlsx_diff::run now leaves out records/, where attempt 1 found the P1
    #     log quoting the P1 script.
    # 10. Rerun at the third code commit (planning's message: the P2 negative
